WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.668
A random variable assigns a number to each outcome of a random experiment

00:00:05.668 --> 00:00:09.721
For two independent fair coin tosses, let X count heads

00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:15.800
Different outcomes can receive the same numerical value: head-tail and tail-head both map to one.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.379
Each ordered coin outcome has probability one quarter, but the values of X are not equally likely

00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:30.803
Zero heads has one supporting outcome, one head has two, and two heads has one

00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:33.800
Grouping outcomes creates the variable's probability distribution.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.580
A new numerical rule creates another random variable

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:45.540
If Y equals twice X minus one, its possible values are negative one, one, and three

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:52.500
The mapping changes the numerical labels while preserving the probabilities attached to these three distinct groups.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.556
An event about a random variable corresponds to a collection of original outcomes

00:00:59.556 --> 00:01:06.197
At least one head includes head-head, head-tail, and tail-head, so its probability is three quarters

00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:11.067
Keep the outcome, its numerical value, and its probability conceptually separate.
